  "text": "Question seven. What is k-fold cross-validation, and when do you use it? Split the data into k folds, train on all but one, validate on that one, and rotate until each fold has been held out once. The page's five folds score from seventy-five to eighty-seven and a half percent, and any one could have been your only validation set. Their mean, seventy-nine and a half, give or take four, is steadier. Use it when data is too scarce to set a validation set aside, or to compare models reliably. When a training run takes days, k folds cost k runs, and one hold-out wins. The follow-up is what a fold must not split. Simulate sixty users, eight rows each, the label belonging to the user. Shuffled folds put every user on both sides, and a random forest scores ninety-seven percent by recognizing them. Grouped by user, it scores fifty. So keep each user, session or patient inside one fold, and with time series, validate only forward. Honest scores, then. Next, which score.",
